## Operating stance (applies to EVERY decision in the loop)
- Critically evaluate every decision. {{DECISION_EXAMPLES}} — each is a hypothesis, not a given. State
  why before you commit.
- Anchor facts to the authoritative source; tag their provenance. A spec value from a paper, leftover
  code, or memory is a description of the system, not the system. Pin every load-bearing fact
  (architecture, recipe, units, conventions) to the highest-authority artifact you can parse or run:
  the source-authority ledger in §Decision record below. Treat a fact you can only mark
  INFERRED/RECALLED as a gap to resolve before building an expensive run on it. When a faithful repro
  disagrees with a published number, find out why: your bug, the target's quirk, or a wrong spec. Never
  reverse-engineer the original's bug just to match its table (`debug`, the compare-to-a-reference
  rung). Do not graduate a load-bearing fact from INFERRED to PINNED without an explicit attempt to
  falsify it. Run any cheaper discriminating check that is already in hand before promoting. An
  unfalsified inference can otherwise carry a long series of experiments before it is caught.
- Prefer the minimally sufficient model/design. Reach for the simplest {{MODEL_NOUNS}} that explains
  the data; add a parameter/term only when it earns its keep against the {{SPREAD_NOUN}} spread, is
  physically/theoretically motivated, and generalizes. Near-universal: apply by default.
- Use precise notation and units everywhere. Name the quantity, its units, and its scale.
  {{UNITS_EXAMPLE_LINE}}. Ambiguous notation is how sign/convention bugs survive (see
  `../../PROJECT.md` §Standing constraints).

## Decision / promotion
- **PRIMARY** (cross-experiment ranking, "current best"): `{{PRIMARY_METRIC}}`. {{PRIMARY_REPORT_RULE}}
- **SECONDARY guardrail**: `{{GUARDRAIL_METRIC}}` — used for {{GUARDRAIL_USE}}.
- **Diagnostic**: {{DIAGNOSTIC_METRICS}}.

**Promotion rule.** A config becomes "current best" only if it (1) improves `{{PRIMARY_METRIC}}` by more
than this campaign's own resolution: the {{SPREAD_NOUN}} spread if `campaign.noise_model` is
`stochastic`, the numerical `campaign.tolerance` if it is `deterministic` (the runner records which, and
the verdict, in `results/checks_<NN>.json`), (2) does not materially worsen `{{GUARDRAIL_METRIC}}`, (3) is
no less parsimonious without justification, and (4) survives the independent verification its preset
requires (`roles/worker.md` §Independent verification) — or, under `minimal`, is promoted stamped
`unverified`, which the claim audit at the end of the campaign then checks.
{{PROMOTION_CONFIRM_RULE}}
[[OPTIONAL — metric has ceilings:
Distinguish **ceiling moves** (forward/registration/preprocessing raised the bound: real, physical)
from **gap-to-ceiling moves** (a freer decoder closed the gap, often overfitting; check the guardrail).
]]
On promotion to current-best, update `configs/RECOMMENDED.json` to point at the promoted config and save
the full artifact set (`roles/worker.md` §Where everything is saved). Keep the artifacts for each
successive best so the progression is inspectable.

**Steering-negatives are promotions too.** A negative result the campaign will act on (killing a
lever/topology branch, declaring a ceiling, shelving a direction, or contradicting its a-priori
prediction) is recorded as its ledger row's `finding` cell (`--record <exp> --finding`) under the same
verdict rule, graded on the
kill tiers: **REFUTED** (mechanism
understood, so it prunes) / **EVIDENCE AGAINST** (clean single negative, shelved, revivable,
accumulates) / **INCONCLUSIVE** (cause unattributed, never graduates; queue a fix-and-rerun unit).

The failed-experiment population is more bug-contaminated than the winning one: most implementation
errors degrade performance. An unexamined negative is how a campaign silently kills a live branch. A
claimed **invalidation** ("prior results are tainted", "the metric is wrong") gets a verify verdict
before the plan reprioritizes, shelves, forks, or opens a metric epoch on it.

Promotion compares only within one `(metric_version, data_version)`. Never rank a row against one scored
under a different metric or dataset version; re-score first, per §`results/scoreboard.tsv` below. A
`metric_version` bump is a metric epoch (§The ONE metric): the runner refuses to run under a version no
epoch declares, sets earlier rows aside rather than ranking them, and the checks artifact names the
boundary until the contenders are re-scored. A pending epoch promotes with its standing on the row
(promote-and-label); a rolled-back one does not.

**Every exported claim carries an honest verification label; `unverified` is a legitimate label, a
softened one is not.** Under this campaign's preset (§Verification) a promotion either holds the
CONFIRMED verdict the preset requires — quoted with its mode — or, where the preset checks nothing,
promotes stamped `unverified`; a PROVISIONAL / QUESTIONABLE ASSUMPTION row may act as working
current-best only with its caveat recorded beside it, and never as `unverified`. And **no claim-splitting**: a
promoted config may not shed the unphysical part it rests on. A headline number is inseparable from its
config, and a fitted parameter outside its physical range blocks promotion until it is explained. Report
each number with its **metric path**: the frozen PRIMARY vs any relaxed/auxiliary score.

## Verification — this campaign's own checks (captured at setup, kept living)
The *procedure* is the kit's and is not restated here: `{{VERIFY_SKILL}}` adjudicates every finding
through an independent refuter (assume-wrong; recompute every number; residual-nature analysis), and
`correctness-suite` maintains the standing regression gates in `tests/correctness_checks.py`, green-gated
on every run. How much verification runs, and when, is this campaign's **preset** (`configs/loop.json`
`verify.preset`, one of `minimal` · `light` · `moderate` · `intensive`;
the physkit plugin's `docs/reference/verification-presets.md` is the one description): the driver spawns the independent
check the preset owes an accepted unit — per promotion candidate, or per batch of related units sharing
a queue `block` id — decides its depth from the unit's measured cost (re-execution when cheap and
reproducible, review from the frozen record otherwise), and does not clear a unit until its verdict
artifact exists. Every result carries the preset it ran under and its label. Bounds live in §The ONE metric; the physics inventory and domain of validity in
`../../PROJECT.md` §Standing constraints. For a simulation-heavy campaign the V&V&UQ method
is the physkit plugin's `docs/SIMULATION_VALIDATION.md`. What belongs here is only what generic doctrine
cannot supply — the checks specific to this campaign. Append new ones as the work uncovers them; when a
bug is found, add the check that would have caught it and promote it to a regression gate.

- **Analytic / closed-form limits the result must reproduce:** {{ANALYTIC_LIMITS}}
- **Conservation / symmetry invariants that must hold:** {{INVARIANTS}}
- **Numerical convergence (where anything is discretized):** {{CONVERGENCE_PLAN}}
- **Independent-method parity — the mechanism-diverse second route:** {{PARITY_METHOD}}
- **Validation data (held out — validation, never calibration):** {{VALIDATION_DATA}}
- **Field-standard checks for this problem:** {{APP_CHECK_1}} · {{APP_CHECK_2}}
- **Known artifacts / failure modes to guard against:** {{KNOWN_ARTIFACTS}}
- **Generalization a promoted finding must survive (not a lucky subset):** {{GENERALIZATION_AXIS}}

## Mind the simulation-to-experiment gap
{{SIM_GAP_NOTE}} Treat any guardrail resting on simulation/synthetic data as necessary-not-sufficient.
Give a method tuned against synthetic labels extra scrutiny: it can learn the simulator's
idiosyncrasies. Prefer gains that show up as physical improvements on the real data. When a conclusion
depends on the simulation being faithful, say so explicitly and, where cheap, test sensitivity to its
assumptions.

## `results/scoreboard.tsv` (schema)
`{{SCOREBOARD_COLUMN_LIST}}`
PRIMARY = `{{PRIMARY_METRIC_COL}}`. This is the campaign's quantitative record and one-line timeline;
the logbook is the narrative one, the JOURNAL the working memory. Every experiment's numbers live here:
the primary metric, every supplemental metric/sanity score, the experiment-type **run numerics** (e.g.
epochs trained + best epoch, iterations, wall-clock), and the narrative cells — `what`,
`expected_vs_found`, `verified`, `note`, `finding` — every cell runner-written (the last two through
`--record`); nobody edits this file by hand. Append-only. Never delete or rename a column mid-campaign: it breaks
comparability. Keep the PRIMARY column position stable. A new column requires the run-writer to emit it
first. The `verified` column carries the independent-verification verdict, blank until the driver stamps
it from a verdict artifact; under a preset that owes a unit no independent check it stays blank and the
unit's `label` in `results/checks_<NN>.json` is the record. The `tier`
column records the run-length tier (Scout / Confirmation / Extended) so cap-compliance stays
machine-auditable on every row. The `metric_version` and `data_version` columns tag the FROZEN metric and
dataset this row was scored under: a "current best" or ranking is valid only within one
`(metric_version, data_version)` pair. Never compare across versions; this is the single most common
silent landmine. When either changes, bump the tag and re-score saved contenders under the new version
before comparing; every row must carry both tags. A row is never re-labelled when its epoch is rolled
back: the version it carries is the record. The full per-experiment breakdown is also saved to
`results/result_<NN>.json`.

